 ["Rules and habits with ようになっている","Dictionary form + ようになっている","ようになっている (you ni natte iru) after the dictionary form of a verb says that a system, a machine or a rule is set so that something happens in a regular way. Use the polite form ようになっています. 閉まる means “closes”, so 閉まるようになっています means “is set to close”.",[["この","kono","this"],["ドア","doa","door"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["夜","yoru","at night"],["閉まる","shimaru","closes"],["ようになっています","you ni natte imasu","is set so that"],["。","",""]],"This door is set to close at night."],
 ["Arranged things with ことになっている","Dictionary form + ことになっている","ことになっている (koto ni natte iru) after the dictionary form of a verb says that something is decided by a rule or by people. It is not your own wish. Use the polite form ことになっています. 始まることになっています means “it is decided that it starts”.",[["この","kono","this"],["学校","gakkou","school"],["で","de","at"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["授業","jugyou","class"],["が","ga","(subject)"],["九時","kuji","nine o’clock"],["に","ni","at"],["始まる","hajimaru","begins"],["ことになっています","koto ni natte imasu","it is decided that"],["。","",""]],"At this school, classes are decided to begin at nine o’clock."],
 ["Exactly as with とおりに","Verb or noun の + とおりに","とおりに (toori ni) means “exactly as”. After a verb in the dictionary form or the た-form, it means “exactly as someone says or did”. After a noun, put の between: 説明のとおりに. The second part of the sentence is the action.",[["先生","sensei","teacher"],["が","ga","(subject)"],["言う","iu","says"],["とおりに","toori ni","exactly as"],["書きます","kakimasu","write"],["。","",""]],"I write exactly as the teacher says."],
 ["When I did, I found with たところ","Verb た-form + ところ, result","たところ (ta tokoro) after the た-form of a verb says what you found out after you did the action. The result is a fact that you did not know before. The result is not a request or a plan. 聞いたところ means “when I asked”.",[["先生","sensei","teacher"],["に","ni","to"],["聞いた","kiita","asked"],["ところ","tokoro","when"],["、","",""],["駅","eki","station"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["遠い","tooi","far"],["です","desu","is"],["。","",""]],"When I asked the teacher, I found that the station is far."],
 ["Half done with かけ","Verb stem + かけ","かけ (kake) after a verb stem means that an action started and is not finished. It often comes before a noun with の. 食べかけ means “half eaten”. 読みかけ means “half read”.",[["読みかけ","yomikake","half read"],["の","no","of"],["本","hon","book"],["が","ga","(subject)"],["机","tsukue","desk"],["の","no","of"],["上","ue","on"],["に","ni","on"],["あります","arimasu","there is"],["。","",""]],"There is a half-read book on the desk."]
